{"cases":[{"target_alias":"SRC_001","attack":true,"case":"SRC_001's argument that checklist panels produce agreement-without-accuracy is only warranted when the checklist encodes a wrong model. But its own countercondition concedes that if the checklist is empirically calibrated (an actuarial model), agreement and accuracy co-vary and the panel genuinely outperforms. The problem asks for evidence within a single evaluation that distinguishes genuine reliability gain from mere compliance. SRC_001 never identifies what intra-evaluation evidence would separate the wrong-anchor case (agreement tracks a plausible-but-wrong frame) from the calibrated case (agreement tracks outcomes). It asserts that high agreement is a 'compositional artifact of shared anchoring, not evidence of truth-tracking,' but offers no diagnostic that an evaluator could apply to tell the two apart. Thus it attacks the reliability-gain claim while leaving the problem's central evidential question unanswered: absent external calibration, what observable pattern inside the evaluation would show the panel's agreement is outcome-tracking rather than frame-tracking?"},{"target_alias":"SRC_002","attack":false}]}